{"id": "2946", "revid": "2363356", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=2946", "title": "Bathinda", "text": "Bathinda is a city in Punjab, India known for its ancient fort, its deep ties to Sikh history and its role as a major agricultural and transport hub in southern Punjab.\nUnderstand.\nBathinda is one of Punjab\u2019s oldest continuously inhabited cities, set in the Malwa region on wide, flat plains shaped by centuries of agriculture and canal irrigation. Its location has always made it a crossroads: ancient trade routes passed through here, medieval armies fought for control of its fort, and modern highways and rail lines now link southern Punjab to the rest of India. The city\u2019s name appears in early records as Tabarhindh, Batrind, and Bikramgarh, reflecting its long strategic history.\nThe city\u2019s most recognisable landmark is Qila Mubarak, a large brick fort with origins going back to the Kushana period. Over time it has witnessed invasions, political struggles and royal stories, including the imprisonment of Razia Sultan, the first woman to rule the Delhi Sultanate. The neighbourhoods around the fort still have narrow lanes, old shrines and traditional markets that reflect Bathinda\u2019s layered past.\nBathinda also has a major place in Sikh history. At nearby Talwandi Sabo, Guru Gobind Singh prepared the final version of the Guru Granth Sahib, leading to the establishment of Takht Sri Damdama Sahib, one of Sikhism\u2019s five temporal seats. Pilgrims visit throughout the year, giving the area a steady, peaceful rhythm.\nAgriculture shapes much of Bathinda\u2019s identity. Wheat, cotton and sugarcane dominate the surrounding countryside, supported by canal networks built during the British period. In recent decades the city has grown into a regional centre with universities, hospitals, refineries and military establishments.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nBathinda is well connected via train to other cities of India like New Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Jaipur, Ahmedabad, etc. There are frequent trains from Delhi.\nBy bus.\nBathinda is well connected via roads to other cities of India like New Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Jaipur, Ahmedabad, etc. There are frequent buses from Delhi.\nGet around.\nBathinda is a straightforward city to navigate, with wide roads, a grid\u2011like layout in newer areas and a compact historic core around Qila Mubarak. Most travellers rely on a mix of auto\u2011rickshaws, cycle\u2011rickshaws and local buses, all of which are easy to find and inexpensive. Autos are the quickest way to move between neighbourhoods, while cycle\u2011rickshaws are useful for short hops inside the old city\u2019s narrow lanes.\nTaxis and app\u2011based cabs operate in Bathinda, though availability can vary depending on the time of day. They\u2019re convenient for longer trips, such as reaching Talwandi Sabo or the thermal plant area. Local buses run on major routes and connect the city with nearby towns, but they can be crowded during peak hours.\nBathinda Junction railway station sits close to the city centre, making onward travel simple. The main markets, bus stands and several hotels are within a short auto\u2011ride. Traffic is generally manageable, though evenings around the markets can get busy. The city is flat, so walking is comfortable in cooler months, especially around the fort and older bazaars.\nDo.\nBathinda also has a large lake area, including Bathinda Lake and the thermal plant lake, where people go for quiet walks, sunsets and birdwatching. The city\u2019s parks and open spaces make it pleasant to wander in cooler months. For a simple local experience, spending time in the evening markets or trying Malwa dishes like makki di roti and saag can be enjoyable.\nIf you\u2019re interested in industry or modern development, Bathinda\u2019s refinery zone and new university areas show the city\u2019s growing side, though they\u2019re more functional than scenic. Overall, Bathinda is best for travellers who enjoy history, religious sites and relaxed city exploration rather than fast\u2011paced sightseeing.\nEat.\nYou\u2019ll find plenty of dhabas, small family restaurants and sweet shops across the city, especially around the old markets and the main roads leading toward the bus stand and railway station.\nStay safe.\nThe busiest parts of town are around the railway station, bus stand and the markets near Qila Mubarak, where crowds can get dense. Keeping your belongings close and avoiding carrying valuables openly is a simple precaution that works well.\nTraffic can be hectic at peak hours, especially on the main roads leading toward the refinery and the bus stand. Crossing streets carefully and using pedestrian crossings where available helps, since drivers may not always slow down. At night, central areas remain active, but quieter neighbourhoods can feel isolated, so it\u2019s better to use autos or cabs rather than walking long distances.\nBathinda\u2019s climate can be extreme. Summers are very hot, so staying hydrated and avoiding long walks in the afternoon is important. Winters can be foggy, which affects visibility on roads; if you\u2019re travelling early in the morning, allow extra time and choose reliable transport.\nThe city is safe for visitors, and people are generally helpful. If you\u2019re heading to Talwandi Sabo or rural areas, roads are straightforward but can be slow due to tractors and local traffic. Staying patient and following local advice keeps things easy."}
